## Why This Is Asked

This question tests cross-boundary bit manipulation and 128-bit arithmetic, which are fundamental skills in cryptography and high-performance kernel development. It also evaluates the ability to implement rotation operations without branching logic.

## Key Concepts

- 128-bit rotation across two 64-bit limbs
- Using a single 128-bit intermediate simplifies boundary handling
- Edge-case handling via rotation count masking (eliminates branches)

## Code Example

```cpp
void rotl128(uint64_t hi, uint64_t lo, unsigned int k, uint64_t *out_hi, uint64_t *out_lo) {
  __uint128_t v = ((__uint128_t)hi << 64) | lo;
  k &= 127;
  __uint128_t r = (v << k) | (v >> (128 - k));
  *out_hi = (uint64_t)(r >> 64);
  *out_lo = (uint64_t)r;
}
```

## Edge Cases

- `k = 0`: Returns original value unchanged
- `k = 64`: Swaps hi and lo parts completely

## Why This Is Asked
Rotations are fundamental in cryptography (e.g., SHA-2), hashing, and bitboard manipulation. Implementing them correctly on composite 128-bit types tests understanding of cross-word boundaries and careful handling of edge cases.

## Key Concepts
- Decompose 128-bit value into hi/lo 64-bit halves
- Handle k near 64 where rotation crosses half boundaries
- No loops required—just careful bitwise arithmetic
- Return early for k==0 to avoid undefined behavior with 64-bit shifts

## Code Example
```c
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128;

uint128 rotl128(uint128 x, unsigned k) {
    k &= 127;
    if (k == 0) return x;

    if (k < 64) {
        uint64_t hi = (x.hi << k) | (x.lo >> (64 - k));
        uint64_t lo = (x.lo << k) | (x.hi >> (64 - k));
        return (uint128){hi, lo};
    } else {
        unsigned k2 = k - 64;
        uint64_t hi = (x.lo << k2) | (x.hi >> (64 - k2));
        uint64_t lo = (x.hi << k2) | (x.lo >> (64 - k2));
        return (uint128){hi, lo};
    }
}
```

